@233 = ~Galeno the Staff~

@234 = ~Galeno was a clever thief. Over the course of his adventures, he learned to imitate many people, including wizards and sorcerers. His imitation was so skillful and complete that he could use their magical wands and staves. Staves were of little use to him, however: he did not know how to wield them as weapons, and they were difficult to carry while scaling the walls of Vurkis's temples, for Galeno loved stealing from the followers of that evil god. Wands, on the other hand, could be slipped into a pouch or pocket, or sewn into the lining of a cloak, and Galeno found them extremely useful.

One day, while fleeing the guardians of a temple of Vurkis, Galeno pulled a wand from beneath his cloak, intending to create the illusion of a wall between himself and his pursuers. Instead, he turned one of the guards into a chicken! Forced to leap from a window rather than make a more dignified escape, Galeno landed in a street cleaner's cart. The foul-smelling contents cushioned his fall, but also ruined his clothes.

Frustrated and temporarily humiliated, Galeno searched for a better way to organize and carry his collection of wands. After several experiments, he eventually fixed them to a hollow tube of thick paper wrapped around his forearm. The wands inside the tube protruded far enough from the back for him to tell them apart. He then set out to rob a sorcerer named Joaquin Mago, whose arrogant display of wealth simply begged to be plundered.

Alas, the sorcerer caught Galeno in the middle of the burglary. To his horror, the entire thing had been a trap! The guard Galeno had turned into a chicken was Joaquin's son, and the ostentatious display of wealth had been nothing more than bait intended to lure a greedy—but clever—thief. Galeno and Joaquin fought a tremendous duel. As the battle raged, Galeno's concentration faltered and a strange confluence occurred between the wands and the sorcerer's magic. The tube around Galeno's arm began to twist and lengthen. The wands started to merge with him, and despite all his efforts he could not free himself from the transformation. Desperate, Galeno begged Joaquin to help him.

The sorcerer's heart softened. After all, his son had only remained a chicken temporarily. Joaquin tried to stop the transformation, but he could neither halt nor reverse it. The strange mass continued to twist and change, Galeno and the wands endlessly mingling and merging together. Unable to control or contain what Galeno was becoming, Joaquin finally hurled the ever-changing mass through the dimensions.

Many years later, Dillpater found what remained of the clever thief. By then, Galeno had been completely transformed, his body and his wands now forming a single magical object.

STATISTICS:

Combat Ability:
- Creates the effect of a random wand on hit (no projectile or area effect)

Charge Ability:
- Creates a random wand

THAC0: +3
Damage: 1D6+3
Damage Type: Crushing
Speed Factor: 1
Proficiency Type: Quarterstaff
Type: Two-handed
Requires: Strength 5

Weight: 3~

