@2941 = ~Spirit of the Paragon of Bovine Vengeance~

@295 = ~Dillpater's Grimoire~

@296 = ~This mage's grimoire is written in a language you do not recognize. Even the magical runes used to describe a spell and how to cast it are strangely arranged, as though they came from a land where magic followed different rules. In the final pages, however, the runes are conventional and describe common spells. On the last page, a passage written in Quasi-Common catches your eye:

**Dillpater's Journal — 525th year of the reign of Lord Artificer Omanond**

My apprentices stole my grimoire.

MY grimoire.

I had to create this one. It is only a copy. Incomplete.

They set out adventuring through the lands of Sharn. With MY grimoire. I am going to find them. I will find them. They must be there. They have to be there.

I am taking the Wonderful Discombulator with me. It would be absurd to leave without it. Completely absurd. I need it. Need it.

**Dillpater's Journal — 652nd year of the reign of Lord Artificer Omanond**

I did not find them.

I followed their trail. They entered some kind of series of arenas. Why?

I went in too. I am going to find them. I will recover my grimoire.

I found monsters. Traps. Mages. Things that should never have existed. I survived all of it. I even reached “the final.”

But I did not find them. I did not recover my grimoire.

Impossible. With all my power, and I did not find them. Impossible.

Telim'Gar is the leader. He offered me a place here.

His power resonated with mine. I listened to him.

Perhaps my apprentices will come.

I am waiting for them.

I see other mages arriving. They are not artificers. They are not my apprentices.

The mages die. All of them. Weak.

Their grimoires are ridiculous. Nothing more than a collection of exercises for apprentices.

I collect grimoires. I have too many.

**Dillpater's Journal — 450th year after the Standing Stones, according to Telim'Gar**

I have not seen my apprentices again. I have not recovered my grimoire.

This has gone on too long. I approached this the wrong way. I must proceed differently.

STATISTICS:

Special:
- A particularly intelligent mage can learn ancient, forgotten spells by reading this grimoire.
- These spells from an ancient age are represented in the wizard's spellbook in a completely different way.

Requires: Intelligence 20
Weight: 2~

